End-to-End COVID Data Analytics Pipeline (Kaggle → Python → SQL Server)

This project demonstrates a full analytics workflow using publicly available global COVID-19 data. The objective was to simulate a real-world data pipeline by ingesting raw data, cleaning and engineering metrics in Python, and structuring the data inside SQL Server for analysis and reporting.

Project Summary:
-Ingested raw COVID-19 dataset from Kaggle into Jupyter Notebook

-Performed data cleaning and validation using pandas (handled scientific notation, validated numeric types, standardized fields)
-Engineered derived metrics including:
  -Case Fatality Rate
  -Active Case Rate
  -Cases per 100k population (normalized metrics)

-Loaded the cleaned dataset into SQL Server using SQLAlchemy and pyodbc

-Applied SQL transformations including:

  -Phase 1 — Data Cleaning & Standardization
    -Created a dedicated SQL Server database (CovidDB) and validated the dataset before transformations.
    -Cleaned text fields by removing leading/trailing whitespace from country and continent.
    -Removed non-country rollup rows by deleting records where continent = 'All'.
    -Identified abbreviated country names using string-length filtering and standardized them into full names (USA → United States, UK → United Kingdom, UAE → United Arab Emirates, DRC → Democratic Republic of Congo, CAR → Central African Republic, DPRK → North Korea) using nested REPLACE() logic.

  Phase 2 — Table Enhancements & Metric Engineering
    -Built continent-level summaries by aggregating total_cases across continents.
    -Added a case_rank column and populated it using a window function (RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total_cases DESC)) to rank countries by total cases.
    -Added a Risk_category column and assigned risk tiers using 
      -CASE logic:
        High: > 1,000,000 cases
        Medium: > 100,000 cases
        Low: otherwise

    -Calculated and permanently stored two key rate metrics:
      -case_fatality_rate = (total_deaths / total_cases)
      -active_case_rate = (active_cases / total_cases)
        -Then converted both to whole-numbers by multiplying by 100 and rounding.

-Structured the workflow to simulate a production-style analytics pipeline

Technologies Used:
  -Python (pandas, SQLAlchemy)

  -SQL Server (SSMS 22)

  -SQL (aggregation, CASE logic, schema modification)

  -Kaggle (data source)

Key Outcomes:
  -Built a structured dataset optimized for analytical querying
  -Demonstrated end-to-end ownership of data ingestion, transformation, and storage
  -Practiced database-level transformations and schema-level decision making

Produced a cleaned and enriched SQL table (Covid_nums) that supports structured analysis, including standardized country     naming, continent-level rollups, ranked case comparisons, risk segmentation, and percent-based rate metrics for reporting and insights.
